From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 12:08:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: reorder slots for a window rounded up to a power
 of two

A buffered frame's slot is its sequence number modulo the BA window. A
window that doesn't divide 4096 maps two sequence numbers within one
window to the same slot where they wrap (with 63: 4095 and 0), and the
second is freed as a duplicate, though its block ack went out: the
transmitter never sends it again. An ADDBA request may carry any window
up to the maximum.

Round the window up to a power of two for the reorder buffer: the slots
stay apart, and the transmitter still sends no further than the window
it agreed to. mac80211's own reorder buffer has the same arithmetic.

The rx block ack simulation (tests/rx-sim-test.c, a later commit) found
it: with windows of 63 and 48, frames were lost at a wrap and one was
passed on out of order; none with this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 agg-rx.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/agg-rx.c b/agg-rx.c
index 456441aa..251675f2 100644
--- a/agg-rx.c
+++ b/agg-rx.c
@@ -267,6 +267,15 @@ int mt76_rx_aggr_start(struct mt76_dev *dev, struct mt76_wcid *wcid, u8 tidno,
 
 	mt76_rx_aggr_stop(dev, wcid, tidno);
 
+	/*
+	 * A frame's slot is its sequence number modulo the window. Unless the
+	 * window divides 4096 (a power of two), two sequence numbers within
+	 * one window share a slot where they wrap (with 63: 4095 and 0), and
+	 * the second is dropped as a duplicate though the transmitter has its
+	 * block ack. A window rounded up keeps them apart; the transmitter
+	 * still sends no further than the window it agreed to.
+	 */
+	size = roundup_pow_of_two(size);
 	tid = kzalloc(struct_size(tid, reorder_buf, size), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!tid)
 		return -ENOMEM;
